&quot;...Now, that deathly Ai-no-Yama song was being heard in a deathly mood, and not only by the party of five guests and the troupe around them. One more person was there, listening.&quot;

&quot;Jutting out beyond the courtyard was a half-story room, and unlike the room after room of brightness all around it, this one was strangely gloomy and dim. No wonder. Here were kept women tormented by illness, and women gone mad for their secret lovers,&quot;

&quot;and since the place served as rest-cure and lock-up both, it was the kind of room whose rumors never took a single day off: that some woman had coughed blood and died here, that a ghost appeared.&quot;

&quot;Before the ballad began, a woman in this room had ground her ink till it flowed, unrolled a roll of paper already quite long, and was composing a letter.&quot;

&quot;The courtesans of Furuichi did not go in for flashy things like the Katsuyama hairdo and the shikake. They kept to a plain, ordinary-woman style, and on some women that look worked remarkably well,&quot;

&quot;so stories were not rare of a younger man staking his very life in love for a mature woman in a white waist-wrap.&quot;

&quot;The woman now writing the letter was likewise suffering from illness, but her plain style showed her character so well that no one could have taken her for what she was.&quot;

&quot;Beneath a vermilion square lantern her brush ran, then held back, and from time to time she wept. And then came the earlier verse—&quot;

　　　　　　&quot;The bells at dusk and dawn
　　　　　　　Cry, &#x27;jakumetsu iraku,&#x27;
　　　　　　　Yet none who hear are startled.&quot;

&quot;Her half-written brush slipped from her fingers. She held perfectly still, listening, as Otama&#x27;s instrumental interlude went streaming and scattering in patterns, like woven silk.&quot;

　　　　　　&quot;Though flowers scatter, spring will bloom.
　　　　　　　Though birds go home to their old nests,
　　　　　　　The road to death, once gone, has no way back.&quot;
